  "cooked": "<p>In total, my nodes seem to have gained around 220 IPv4 peers.</p>\n<p><div class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"><a class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://bnoc.xyz/uploads/default/original/1X/997c73e4cc681a7927091884d148b705eef9e0b4.png\" data-download-href=\"https://bnoc.xyz/uploads/default/997c73e4cc681a7927091884d148b705eef9e0b4\" title=\"image\"><img src=\"https://bnoc.xyz/uploads/default/original/1X/997c73e4cc681a7927091884d148b705eef9e0b4.png\" alt=\"image\" data-base62-sha1=\"lTNGSOIp5GdRqIdWpoH4QTeG6EY\" width=\"567\" height=\"314\"><div class=\"meta\"><svg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#far-image\"></use></svg><span class=\"filename\">image</span><span class=\"informations\">567\u00d7314 13.8 KB</span><svg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#discourse-expand\"></use></svg></div></a></div></p>\n<p>However, I\u2019ve also noticed a small increase of 15 in onion peers. I don\u2019t think bitprojects had any nodes on Tor, but some of the nodes looking for new outbounds might have picked onions to connect to.</p>\n<p><div class=\"lightbox-wrapper\"><a class=\"lightbox\" href=\"https://bnoc.xyz/uploads/default/original/1X/335d56a83ab117070b1c8edc6d31fb4b4d96e465.png\" data-download-href=\"https://bnoc.xyz/uploads/default/335d56a83ab117070b1c8edc6d31fb4b4d96e465\" title=\"image\"><img src=\"https://bnoc.xyz/uploads/default/original/1X/335d56a83ab117070b1c8edc6d31fb4b4d96e465.png\" alt=\"image\" data-base62-sha1=\"7kokk7Q66fwdocR05PJYsMn8WX3\" width=\"563\" height=\"309\"><div class=\"meta\"><svg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-far-image svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#far-image\"></use></svg><span class=\"filename\">image</span><span class=\"informations\">563\u00d7309 16.9 KB</span><svg class=\"fa d-icon d-icon-discourse-expand svg-icon\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><use href=\"#discourse-expand\"></use></svg></div></a></div></p>\n<p>Don\u2019t really notice something on the other networks. Possibly 10 new I2P peers, but there seems to be a lot of churn anyways.</p>",
